    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d577">

                <pb facs="0563" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 862.20235/202</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-12-17T17:00:00-04:16">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 17, 1917, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8.33 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your December 17, 6 p.m., my December 15, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d577-f1"/>
                    Saguier telephoned this afternoon that the President would not come to his
                    office and therefore could not see me to-day. I see no reason to differ from the
                    general belief here that the President is determined not to break with Germany.
                    In the first place he has stated to me, although he had already read the Luxburg
                    telegrams, that he would [not] break with Germany on account of anything that
                    has occurred hitherto. It must further be remarked that no ship has been
                    permitted to clear for the war zone under the Argentine flag since the <hi
                        rendition="#italic"> Pueyrredon;</hi> see my November 22, 11 a.m.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d577-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p> Saguier tells me that the Luxburg telegrams were the work of a madman, and it is
                    now given out from government circles that Luxburg, who is in a German
                    sanitarium, is insane.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d577-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d577-f1" n="1" xml:id="d577-f1-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d577-f2" n="1" xml:id="d577-f2-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d577-f3" n="2" xml:id="d577-f3-content">
                <p>After much correspondence on the subject, Count Luxburg finally (May 9, 1918)
                    sailed for Germany under safe-conducts from the American and British
                    Governments.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
